# Template for /etc/gardyn/light.env — the grow light's daily photoperiod,
# read by light_scheduler.py inside mqtt.service (T-527).
#
#     sudo install -d -m 0755 /etc/gardyn
#     sudo install -m 0644 services/etc/gardyn/light.env.example \
#          /etc/gardyn/light.env
#     sudoedit /etc/gardyn/light.env        # optional — the values below work
#
# No restart is needed after an edit. The scheduler re-reads this file on every
# tick, so a change takes effect within one tick (30 s as shipped).
#
# THIS FILE IS OPTIONAL, AND THAT IS THE OPPOSITE OF netwatch.env.example NEXT
# TO IT. Read that one's header and then read this paragraph, because the two
# policies contradict each other on purpose:
#
#   gardyn-netwatch REBOOTS THE HOST. A watchdog aimed at somebody else's LAN
#   is worse than no watchdog, so it has no defaults and refuses to run on any
#   incomplete config.
#
#   This one drives a LAMP. A grow light that refuses to run is a dark garden,
#   which is the exact failure T-527 exists to remove — and a photoperiod is
#   not site-specific the way LAN addressing is, so a built-in default is safe
#   to ship in a public repository. Every failure here (file absent, key
#   missing, value malformed) is logged loudly and falls back to the schedule
#   compiled into light_schedule.py's DEFAULT_SCHEDULE, which is the values
#   below verbatim.
#
# Do not "fix" that asymmetry into a refusal to match netwatch.
#
# The file is read by the scheduler itself, so `KEY=value` lines, `#` comments,
# blank lines, a leading `export`, and surrounding quotes are all accepted.
# It is also a valid systemd EnvironmentFile= if you ever want it there too.

# The photoperiod, as `HH:MM=BRIGHTNESS` boundaries separated by commas.
# Brightness is a whole percent, 0-100; 0 is off.
#
# EACH ENTRY OPENS A PHASE THAT RUNS UNTIL THE NEXT ONE, and the last entry of
# the day wraps around midnight into the first. With the values below the lamp
# is off from 19:00 through 03:00, at 50% for the 03:00 and 18:00 ramps, and at
# 100% between 04:00 and 18:00. There is no "off" entry needed at midnight: the
# 19:00 boundary is still in force at 01:30.
#
# A TYPO IS REFUSED, NOT INTERPRETED. Hours and minutes must be two digits
# ("3:00" and "003:00" are both rejected rather than read as 03:00), a trailing
# comma is an error rather than an ignored empty entry, two boundaries at the
# same time are refused because which one wins would be undefined, and anything
# after the brightness ("03:00=50 50") is refused rather than silently dropped.
# Every refusal falls back to the built-in schedule and says so in the journal:
#
#     journalctl -u mqtt -g 'Schedule:'
#
# These are the values this garden has been running since long before T-527,
# taken from the Home Assistant automation that used to own them.
GARDYN_LIGHT_SCHEDULE=03:00=50,04:00=100,18:00=50,19:00=0

# Optional. What to drive the lamp to when the clock cannot be trusted AND
# there is no remembered brightness to hold — i.e. a first boot with no
# network, on a Pi that has no RTC.
#
# It is 100 rather than 0 deliberately. The alternatives were considered in the
# T-527 design and this is the recorded decision: refusing to drive would leave
# the garden dark for the whole outage, "degrading INTO the failure mode this
# work exists to avoid rather than away from it". A lamp lit at the wrong hour
# costs the plants nothing by comparison.
#
# This is a SAFETY FLOOR rather than a site-specific fact, so omitting the key
# inherits the built-in value (100) rather than being an error.
GARDYN_LIGHT_UNSYNCED_FALLBACK=100

# WHEN IS THE CLOCK "UNTRUSTED"? Less often than you would guess, and this
# paragraph exists because an earlier version of the one above said the window
# was "the few seconds until NTP lands", which was wrong in a way that cost a
# blocked deploy (T-527.19).
#
# `NTPSynchronized`, the systemd property the scheduler asks, does NOT mean
# "the clock is accurate". systemd computes it as "the kernel's accumulated
# error bound is under 16 seconds", and the kernel widens that bound by 500 µs
# every second that NTP is unreachable — so a PERFECTLY ACCURATE clock is
# reported as unsynchronised about 8.9 hours into any network outage. Measured
# on this Pi, not inferred.
#
# So the scheduler does not ask that question twice. Once it has seen a real
# synchronisation, it trusts the clock for the life of the process: a clock
# that synced this boot and has since free-run is out by the crystal's drift,
# which is seconds, not by the length of the outage. Only a process that has
# NEVER seen a sync — a boot with no network, where the clock was restored from
# /var/lib/systemd/timesync/clock and may be days stale — holds or falls back,
# and even then only for two hours, after which it follows the schedule
# regardless. Two hours of holding beats a dark garden; so does a photoperiod
# shifted by an outage.
#
# None of that is configurable from this file on purpose — it is safety policy,
# not site configuration. It lives in light_scheduler.py as
# NEVER_SYNCED_HOLD_SECONDS, with the reasoning beside it.
